@Moonj64 Thanks for filing an issue!  I looked into this and was unable to reproduce the issue with a daily task.  I tried both through the UI (checking a daily task as done) and using the service call like you provided in the description. In both cases the task instance is marked completed in todoist and a new recurrence is scheduled.  That new recurrence for the daily task continues to appear in the todo list in the UI as well as the calendar.  

It's possible that perhaps you didn't see the task re-appear before the entity updated again?  I believe the interval is set to update every 1 minute from the API.

Let me know if there are more specific instructions that I might have missed in order to reproduce this issue.

@allenporter I was wondering if you had any thoughts on how to fix this.  

It looks like [this change](https://github.com/home-assistant/core/pull/106208/files#diff-409a99b7c89f7762d674618be0d816977e081295a329f5228ece166f2d454973) (In particular sending the correct keys for `due_date` and `due_datetime`) causes us to send one of the `due_*` parameters on every request when we update a task.  This ends up overwriting recurring date information.  Then after the update if the status was changed to `completed` it calls `close_task`.  Since this task is no longer recurring, it fully closes the task and todoist won't schedule the next recurrence since we overwrote that data in the previous api call.

I realize part of the problem is that HA todo items don't have a concept of recurrence at the moment.  One thing that we might be able to do is to only send one of the `due_*` params if the due date/datetime was specified in the service call.  At the moment that is always sent to the api even if it wasn't specified in the service call.  This still won't fix the issue of losing recurrence data if a user changes the due date/datetime, but that seems reasonable to me at the moment.
